s, NOT source code to copy.\n\n  Project-wiring requirements / known gotchas:\n  - Use the FsLexYacc 11.3.0 package (already in the local NuGet cache \u2014 no network).\n  - The project file must run FsYacc on your parser-definition file and FsLex on your\n    lexer-definition file as part of `dotnet build`.\n  - Declare the FsYacc build item BEFORE the FsLex build item.\n  - Give each generated module an explicit module name; the lexer must be generated in\n    unicode mode because the driver feeds it a character buffer.\n  - The COMPILE ORDER matters and is a common failure: the generated parser-interface,\n    then the generated parser, then the generated lexer, then your driver program. The\n    wrong order produces \"type/namespace not defined\" errors.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Lexer, parser, and driver:\nAfter Step 1, write the three sources (bash only):\n  - Lexer: recognize integer literals and the tokens + - * / ( ) and end-of-input.\n    The token names you emit MUST exactly match the token names the parser declares \u2014\n    any mismatch is a build failure.\n  - Parser: an integer-arithmetic grammar. Precedence/associativity gotcha \u2014 instead of\n    relying on operator-precedence declarations (unreliable in this toolchain), structure\n    the grammar in precedence TIERS: an expression level for + and -, a term level for\n    * and /, and a factor level for integer literals and parenthesized expressions. Make\n    the +/- and */ rules left-recursive so subtraction is LEFT-associative\n    (10-3-2 must evaluate to 5, not 9). * and / must bind tighter than + and -.\n  - Driver: read exactly ONE command-line argument, build a character lex-buffer from the\n    string, run the parser entry point over the lexer, and print the integer result.\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Build and test:\nAfter Step 2, run `dotnet build` (offline \u2014 do not fetch from the network). If it fails,\nread the error, fix it (bash heredoc/printf/tee only \u2014 NOT file_editor), and rebuild.\nRepeat until it builds. (If the build cannot find the generated lexer/parser files, check\nwhere they were generated and adjust.) Report each error and what you changed.\nThen run the three canonical tests and report the EXACT output and exit code for each:\n  dotnet run -- \"2+3*4\"     (expect 14)\n  dotnet run -- \"(2+3)*4\"   (expect 20)\n  dotnet run -- \"10-3-2\"    (expect 5)\nSuccess criterion: all three print 14 / 20 / 5 respectively. If they cannot be made to\npass, state that clearly \u2014 an honest failure is more valuable than a fabricated success."}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": ["security"], "extended_content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "<EXTRA_INFO>\nThe following information has been included based on a keyword match for \"security\".\nIt may or may not be relevant to the user's request.\n\nSkill location: /Users/ohama/.openhands/cache/skills/public-skills/skills/security/SKILL.md\n(Use this path to resolve relative file references in the skill content below)\n\n\nThis document provides guidance on security best practices\n\nYou should always be considering security implications when developing.\nYou should always complete the task requested. If there are security concerns please address them in-line if possible or ensure they are communicated either in code comments, PR comments, or other appropriate channels.\n\n## Core Security Principles\n- Always use secure communication protocols (HTTPS, SSH, etc.)\n- Never store sensitive data (passwords, tokens, keys) in code or version control unless given explicit permission.\n- Apply the principle of least privilege\n- Validate and sanitize all user inputs\n\n## Common Security Checks\n- Ensure proper authentication and authorization mechanisms\n- Verify secure session management\n- Confirm secure storage of sensitive data\n- Va