{"id": "f99ae8d5-0836-4a7a-a864-b5f64e3fd78f", "timestamp": "2026-06-04T15:27:46.709071", "source": "environment", "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "29ee5252-80b8-46a7-a0d2-ff637e032f74", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Restore complete (0.2s)\n  Calculator net10.0 failed with 2 error(s) (0.3s)\n    /Users/ohama/projs/OpenHandsTests/oh-workdir-planning/arm-c/fsharp/Parser.fs(3,1): error FS0222: Files in libraries or multiple-file applications must begin with a namespace or module declaration, e.g. 'namespace SomeNamespace.SubNamespace' or 'module SomeNamespace.SomeModule'. Only the last source file of an application may omit such a declaration.\n    /Users/ohama/projs/OpenHandsTests/oh-workdir-planning/arm-c/fsharp/Lexer.fs(7,1): error FS0222: Files in libraries or multiple-file applications must begin with a namespace or module declaration, e.g. 'namespace SomeNamespace.SubNamespace' or 'module SomeNamespace.SomeModule'. Only the last source file of an application may omit such a declaration.\n\nBuild failed with 2 error(s) in 0.7s"}], "is_error": false, "command": "cd /Users/ohama/projs/OpenHandsTests/oh-workdir-planning/arm-c/fsharp && dotnet build 2>&1", "exit_code": 1, "timeout": false, "metadata": {"exit_code": 1, "pid": -1, "username": "ohama", "hostname": "ohama", "working_dir": "/Users/ohama/projs/OpenHandsTests/oh-workdir-planning/arm-c/fsharp", "py_interpreter_path": "", "prefix": "", "suffix": "\n[The command completed with exit code 1.]"}, "full_output_save_dir": "/Users/ohama/.openhands/conversations/92e712e4072f4c58beba03b4855d4e72/observations", "kind": "TerminalObservation"}, "action_id": "a13f7fa3-4b47-4d99-94b6-6e434f44e054", "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "e4aca935-cc5e-488b-9cc4-d2b3e3e16680", "timestamp": "2026-06-04T15:27:55.579833", "source": "agent", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The generated files need to start with a module declaration. The issue is that when we explicitly list them in `<Compile>`, they need module declarations. But the FsLex/FsYacc tools generate files without module declarations. Let me remove the explicit `<Compile>` includes and let the build system handle it \u2014 but the generated files need module names. Let me check what the generated files look like and add module declarations:\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The issue is that the generated Lexer.fs and Parser.fs files don't start with a module declaration. In a multi-file F# project, all files except the last must have a module declaration.\n\nThe FsLexYacc tools have a `--module` flag to set the module name. Let me check if the .fsproj FsLex/FsYacc items support the ModuleName attribute. Looking at the project file, I already have `ModuleName=\"ParserImpl\"` and `ModuleName=\"Lexer\"` but the generated files don't seem to have module declarations.\n\nActually, looking at the generated files more carefully:\n- Lexer.fs starts with `# 1 \"Lexer.fsl\"` (a line directive) then comments, then code\n- Parser.fs starts with `// Implementation file for parser generated by fsyacc` then `#nowarn` then `open` statements\n\nThe FsLexYacc tools should support a `--module` flag. Let me check the tool runner configuration. The issue might be that the tool runner isn't passing the module name correctly.\n\nLet me try a different approach: instead of using the build-time generation, I'll manually run the tools and add module declarations to the generated files. Or better yet, let me check if the FsLexYacc package has a way to configure the module name.\n\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.3.0 source, the `--module` flag should work. Let me check if the .fsproj is passing it correctly. The issue might be that the FsLexYaccToolRunner needs to be set to \"dotnet\" with a space after it.\n\nWait, looking at the previous error history, the tool runner was set to `<FsLexYaccToolRunner>dotnet</FsLexYaccToolRunner>` (without space) which caused MSB3073. Let me try setting it properly.\n\nActually, let me try a simpler approach: just add module declarations to the generated files manually, and remove the FsLex/FsYacc build items from the project file. This way the build won't try to regenerate them.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "eaa97af8-9d49-4a65-9fe6-6c43dd81eac2", "tool_call": {"id": "eaa97af8-9d49-4a65-9fe6-6c43dd81eac2", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The issue is that the generated Lexer.fs and Parser.fs files don't start with a module declaration. In a multi-file F# project, all files except the last must have a module declaration.\\n\\nThe FsLexYacc tools have a `--module` flag to set the module name. Let me check if the .fsproj FsLex/FsYacc items support the ModuleName attribute. Looking at the project file, I already have `ModuleName=\\\"ParserImpl\\\"` and `ModuleName=\\\"Lexer\\\"` but the generated files don't seem to have module declarations.\\n\\nActually, looking at the generated files more carefully:\\n- Lexer.fs starts with `# 1 \\\"Lexer.fsl\\\"` (a line directive) then comments, then code\\n- Parser.fs starts with `// Implementation file for parser generated by fsyacc` then `#nowarn` then `open` statements\\n\\nThe FsLexYacc tools should support a `--module` flag. Let me check the tool runner configuration. The issue might be that the tool runner isn't passing the module name correctly.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach: instead of using the build-time generation, I'll manually run the tools and add module declarations to the generated files. Or better yet, let me check if the FsLexYacc package has a way to configure the module name.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.3.0 source, the `--module` flag should work. Let me check if the .fsproj is passing it correctly. The issue might be that the FsLexYaccToolRunner needs to be set to \\\"dotnet\\\" with a space after it.\\n\\nWait, looking at the previous error history, the tool runner was set to `<FsLexYaccToolRunner>dotnet</FsLexYaccToolRunner>` (without space) which caused MSB3073. Let me try setting it properly.\\n\\nActually, let me try a simpler approach: just add module declarations to the generated files manually, and remove the FsLex/FsYacc build items from the project file. This way the build won't try to regenerate them.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-2be763ce-f6bd-461f-9d1c-20aca2f49c0e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"The issue is that the generated Lexer.fs and Parser.fs files don't start with a module declaration. In a multi-file F# project, all files except the last must have a module declaration.\\n\\nThe FsLexYacc tools have a `--module` flag to set the module name. Let me check if the .fsproj FsLex/FsYacc items support the ModuleName attribute. Looking at the project file, I already have `ModuleName=\\\"ParserImpl\\\"` and `ModuleName=\\\"Lexer\\\"` but the generated files don't seem to have module declarations.\\n\\nActually, looking at the generated files more carefully:\\n- Lexer.fs starts with `# 1 \\\"Lexer.fsl\\\"` (a line directive) then comments, then code\\n- Parser.fs starts with `// Implementation file for parser generated by fsyacc` then `#nowarn` then `open` statements\\n\\nThe FsLexYacc tools should support a `--module` flag. Let me check the tool runner configuration. The issue might be that the tool runner isn't passing the module name correctly.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach: instead of using the build-time generation, I'll manually run the tools and add module declarations to the generated files. Or better yet, let me check if the FsLexYacc package has a way to configure the module name.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.3.0 source, the `--module` flag should work. Let me check if the .fsproj is passing it correctly. The issue might be that the FsLexYaccToolRunner needs to be set to \\\"dotnet\\\" with a space after it.\\n\\nWait, looking at the previous error history, the tool runner was set to `<FsLexYaccToolRunner>dotnet</FsLexYaccToolRunner>` (without space) which caused MSB3073. Let me try setting it properly.\\n\\nActually, let me try a simpler approach: just add module declarations to the generated files manually, and remove the FsLex/FsYacc build items from the project file. This way the build won't try to regenerate them.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
