{"id": "11003e70-be2d-4b09-b2ab-936ce908042a", "timestamp": "2026-06-04T15:37:33.800219", "source": "agent", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Now I understand the fslex format. The lexer uses `{` to start a code block and `}` to end it. The `code` rule (line 584) handles the `{ ... }` blocks. Let me look at the original fslex.fsl source to understand the format:\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "\nNow I understand the fslex format. The lexer uses `{` to start a code block and `}` to end it. The `code` rule (line 584) handles the `{ ... }` blocks. Let me look at the original fslex.fsl source to understand the format:\n\nFrom the fslexlex.fs, I can see:\n- State 0 handles initial characters\n- State 11 (action 11) starts the `code` rule when `{` is encountered\n- The `code` rule (line 584) handles `{ ... }` blocks\n- `}` ends a code block (action 1 in the code rule)\n\nThe fslex spec format is:\n1. Top code (optional) - F# code before the rules\n2. Macros (optional) - `#macro name value` directives\n3. Rules - `rule name = pattern1 -> action1 | pattern2 -> action2`\n4. Bottom code (optional) - F# code after the rules\n\nThe key insight is that `{` starts a code block and `}` ends it. The code block can contain F# code.\n\nLooking at the error: `Lexer.fsl(18): error Unexpected character '}'`\n\nThis means the fslex parser doesn't expect `}` on line 18. The issue is likely that the lexer file has a `}` that's not inside a `{ }` code block.\n\nLet me look at the current Lexer.fsl file to understand the issue.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "991efaf5-b500-4908-8a78-f341b393f257", "tool_call": {"id": "991efaf5-b500-4908-8a78-f341b393f257", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"\\nNow I understand the fslex format. The lexer uses `{` to start a code block and `}` to end it. The `code` rule (line 584) handles the `{ ... }` blocks. Let me look at the original fslex.fsl source to understand the format:\\n\\nFrom the fslexlex.fs, I can see:\\n- State 0 handles initial characters\\n- State 11 (action 11) starts the `code` rule when `{` is encountered\\n- The `code` rule (line 584) handles `{ ... }` blocks\\n- `}` ends a code block (action 1 in the code rule)\\n\\nThe fslex spec format is:\\n1. Top code (optional) - F# code before the rules\\n2. Macros (optional) - `#macro name value` directives\\n3. Rules - `rule name = pattern1 -> action1 | pattern2 -> action2`\\n4. Bottom code (optional) - F# code after the rules\\n\\nThe key insight is that `{` starts a code block and `}` ends it. The code block can contain F# code.\\n\\nLooking at the error: `Lexer.fsl(18): error Unexpected character '}'`\\n\\nThis means the fslex parser doesn't expect `}` on line 18. The issue is likely that the lexer file has a `}` that's not inside a `{ }` code block.\\n\\nLet me look at the current Lexer.fsl file to understand the issue.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-3a8f8f36-9821-4315-a91b-0aad72c00bd4", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"\\nNow I understand the fslex format. The lexer uses `{` to start a code block and `}` to end it. The `code` rule (line 584) handles the `{ ... }` blocks. Let me look at the original fslex.fsl source to understand the format:\\n\\nFrom the fslexlex.fs, I can see:\\n- State 0 handles initial characters\\n- State 11 (action 11) starts the `code` rule when `{` is encountered\\n- The `code` rule (line 584) handles `{ ... }` blocks\\n- `}` ends a code block (action 1 in the code rule)\\n\\nThe fslex spec format is:\\n1. Top code (optional) - F# code before the rules\\n2. Macros (optional) - `#macro name value` directives\\n3. Rules - `rule name = pattern1 -> action1 | pattern2 -> action2`\\n4. Bottom code (optional) - F# code after the rules\\n\\nThe key insight is that `{` starts a code block and `}` ends it. The code block can contain F# code.\\n\\nLooking at the error: `Lexer.fsl(18): error Unexpected character '}'`\\n\\nThis means the fslex parser doesn't expect `}` on line 18. The issue is likely that the lexer file has a `}` that's not inside a `{ }` code block.\\n\\nLet me look at the current Lexer.fsl file to understand the issue.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
